{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of Jacob: Joseph.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Genesis Rabbah 84:6) that Joseph resembled Jacob in all his life-events, and it is as though Joseph was the principal of his generations. This midrash holds that Jacob wished to repair the fact that he had not honored his father all those years; therefore he settled in the land of his father's sojournings and reckoned that through this the past too would be repaired, and he sought to dwell in tranquility. Therefore the wrath of Joseph leapt upon him — to receive punishment also for the days during which his father was not honored by his son, who is the principal of his seed: for the number of those days, Jacob would be un-honored by his son, who is the principal of his generations. Therefore it says ״אֵלֶּה תּוֹלְדוֹת יַעֲקֹב יוֹסֵף״." "And as for what is written, 'the wrath of Joseph leapt upon him' — what is the force of 'leapt'? It should have said, 'the matter of Joseph came upon him.' And what is the force of 'his wrath'? And why attribute wrath to Joseph? And the near explanation to me is this: the wrath of Joseph, that is, the sin of Joseph, by which he angered Elohim in bringing an evil report about his brothers — how he called the brothers slaves — to the point that justice demanded that Joseph be sold as a slave. And if so, even if Jacob had not sought to dwell in tranquility, this punishment would in any case have been fitting to come upon Joseph, except that Joseph was seventeen years old and was not yet liable to punishment in the heavenly court. And because there were joined to the wrath and sin of Joseph also the fact that Jacob sought to dwell in tranquility, therefore HaShem hastened and made Joseph liable to punishment before his time. And this is the force of 'leapt upon him,' for every 'leaping' indicates a thing that is fit to come in any event and is hastened to come before its time. So HaShem desired to bring punishment upon Joseph before his time in order to nullify Jacob's dwelling in tranquility — and this and that together caused it. For the punishment of Joseph alone would not suffice, for he was not liable to punishment, and Jacob's dwelling alone would also not suffice to bring on this account punishment upon Joseph, who had not sinned." "And Rashi brings a parable — to a pearl that fell into the sand, etc. He likened the house of Esav to sand and the house of Jacob to the pearl, along the lines of what is expounded in Vayikra Rabbah (4:6): Esav had six souls and Scripture calls them 'souls' in the plural, because they serve many gods. Jacob had seventy souls and Scripture calls them 'soul,' because they serve one El — end of quotation. And from the side that they serve many gods, strife is constantly found among them and they are not in one accord because of the multiplicity of religions, and they do not unite and join together, like this sand which does not join one to another. But Israel, from the side that they serve one El, peace mediates among them — they are like pearls strung on a thread, held together one to another by means of the thread that connects them, for ״כְּחוּט הַשָּׁנִי שִׂפְתוֹתַיִךְ״ (Song of Songs 4:3) — the lips of knowledge and Torah, which multiply peace in the world, are like this thread that strings the pearls." "<b>And Joseph brought an evil report of them to their father.</b> In all of Scripture we do not find the expression of 'bringing' used with 'dibbah,' but only the expression of 'bringing out.' And even though the commentators said that the expression of 'bringing out' applies to false slander, as it is written (Proverbs 10:18) ״וּמוֹצִיא דִבָּה הוּא כְסִיל״, and likewise (Numbers 13:32) ״וַיּוֹצִיאוּ דִבַּת הָאָרֶץ״, while the expression of 'bringing' applies to something that is true — nevertheless the language is not precise here, for since it says ״דִּבָּתָם״, infer from it that the dibbah is particularized to the brothers, as though the brothers themselves are the subject of the slander. And further, ״אֶל אֲבִיהֶם״ is difficult — it should say ״אֶל אָבִיו״. And further it is difficult: there below it says (Genesis 35:22) ״וַיִּשְׁכַּב אֶת בִּלְהָה פִּילֶגֶשׁ אָבִיו״ — he called her concubine, whereas here it says ״וְהוּא נַעַר אֶת בְּנֵי בִלְהָה וְאֶת בְּנֵי זִלְפָּה נְשֵׁי אָבִיו״ — Scripture calls them outright wives." "But surely all the brothers, the sons of the matriarchs, used to disparage the sons of the maidservants, calling their mothers concubines and maidservants, and their sons they called slaves. And Joseph alone used to associate with the sons of Bilhah and Zilpah, for he said that they are his father's wives outright. And for this reason Reuben disparaged Bilhah, thinking that she is a concubine and not his wife; therefore he violated his bed. And from here is a clear proof that all the sons of the matriarchs used to bring out slander against the sons of the maidservants, calling them slaves. And concerning this it says ״וַיָּבֵא יוֹסֵף אֶת דִּבָּתָם רָעָה״ (Genesis 37:2) — that same evil report which his brothers brought out against the sons of the maidservants, he brought to the father of the sons of Bilhah and Zilpah, for they should have been zealous for the honor of their father not to call his sons slaves. And he reckoned that their father would demand redress for their insult. And this is what it says: ״וּבְטֶרֶם יִקְרַב אֲלֵיהֶם וַיִּתְנַכְּלוּ אֹתוֹ לַהֲמִיתוֹ״ (Genesis 37:18) — for they wanted to kill him from afar by means of a killing arrow before he came near them, measure for measure, for it was he who shot at them the arrow of his tongue that kills even from afar; therefore they too wanted to kill him from afar by means of arrows. And this is what it says (Genesis 49:23) ״וַיִּשְׂטְמֻהוּ בַּעֲלֵי חִצִּים״ — these are his brothers, who wanted to shoot arrows at him. And even though he too spoke evil speech about them, nevertheless ״וַתֵּשֶׁב בְּאֵיתָן קַשְׁתּוֹ״ (Genesis 49:24), for he trod his bow and set it firm with steadfastness, in righteousness and in justice — for behold, it is permitted to speak evil speech about bearers of strife (Yerushalmi Pe'ah 1:1), and his brothers had begun the strife in that they disparaged the sons of the maidservants." "And in the midrash they said concerning the verse ״וּבְטֶרֶם יִקְרַב אֲלֵיהֶם״ that they set the dogs upon him. And the reason for this is that they considered him a slanderer and a talebearer of evil tongue. And our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Pesachim 118a) that everyone who tells evil tongue deserves to be thrown to the dogs, as it says (Exodus 23:1) ״לֹא תִשָּׂא שֵׁמַע שָׁוְא״, and adjacent to it ״לַכֶּלֶב תַּשְׁלִיכוּן אֹתוֹ״. And the reason for this is that everyone who tells evil tongue is likened to a barking dog that slashes with its tongue, and therefore concerning Israel in Egypt, in whom there was no evil tongue, it is written (Exodus 11:7) ״וּלְכֹל בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא יֶחֱרַץ כֶּלֶב לְשֹׁנוֹ״ — measure for measure. And this is what they said: ״נֵלְכָה דֹּתָיְנָה״ — to seek for him the legal strictures according to the Torah that they would instruct you, to cast to the dogs everyone who tells evil tongue. And some say that therefore they set the dogs upon him so that they would eat from him a limb from the living, to repay him for what he said about them — how they ate a limb from the living. And in what follows, the verse ״וּבְטֶרֶם יִקְרַב אֲלֵיהֶם״ will be explained in another way." "And Rashi explained that Yosef told how they ate a limb from the living, and they were suspected of forbidden relations, and they would degrade the sons of the maidservants. And it appears possible to give some support to his words, because it says ״הָיָה רֹעֶה אֶת אֶחָיו בַּצֹּאן״ (Genesis 37:2). Why do I need this? For would it enter your mind that he was tending birds or fish? Rather, it is because we find the term ״רֹעֶה״ in four senses: (1) plain shepherding of flocks; (2) in the context of forbidden relations, as it says (Proverbs 29:3) ״וְרֹעֶה זוֹנוֹת יְאַבֶּד הוֹן״; (3) the term ״רֹעֶה״ applies to those who hold rulership and govern the people, as it is written (Psalms 80:2) ״רֹעֵה יִשְׂרָאֵל הַאֲזִינָה״; (4) the term ״רֹעֶה״ applies to one who goes to feed himself and eat from the flock, as it is written (Ezekiel 34:2) ״הֲלֹא הַצֹּאן יִרְעוּ הָרֹעִים״, and as Rashi explained, with a pointing note on ״אֶת״. And since it says that Yosef was רֹעֶה אֶת אֶחָיו בַּצֹּאן, this implies that his brothers were shepherding in all four matters to which the term ״רֹעֶה״ applies, but he was not in agreement with them except in the flock alone — for if it were not so, let it combine them and place them together: ״יוֹסֵף וְאֶחָיו הָיוּ רוֹעִים בַּצֹּאן״. Rather, surely the term ״אֶת״ serves as the term ״עִם״, and therefore he singled out Yosef from the general to tell you that he did not agree with them except regarding the tending of the flock alone, but his brothers pastured in all of them — for which of them do you infer to exclude? For the verse did not come to obscure but to explain." "And from here Rashi found support for all three: for suspected of forbidden relations — that is ״רֹעֶה זוֹנוֹת״; and ״they ate a limb from the living״ — that the flock pastured them, and they went to feed themselves from the flock to eat in a manner of forbidden food, and that is a limb from the living, which was forbidden to the children of Noach, but other torn flesh was not yet forbidden to them. And behold, this is something learned from its context: just as pasturing of harlotry, or of rulership, or of prohibition — so too this pasturing from the flock is one of prohibition. And ״they would call the sons of the maidservants slaves״ was in order to lord over them, to be over them as shepherds and leaders, saying: We are the sons of the mistresses and you are slaves to us." "And therefore it says ״אֶל אֲבִיהֶם״, to tell you that in all of them there was reason to be sensitive to the honor of their father. For the fact that they called their brothers slaves was something that should have made them sensitive to their father's honor, as explained above; and one who engages in forbidden relations also profanes his father's honor, as it is written (Leviticus 21:29) ״אֶת אָבִיהָ הִיא מְחַלֶּלֶת״, and a proof from what our Sages of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 87:7) that because the image of Yaakov's likeness appeared to Yosef, he was saved from the wife of Potifar; and eating a limb from the living produces cruelty in a person's body, and thereby he violates his father's honor, for it appears as though the cruelty were an acquired trait clinging to him from the side of his offspring, as will be explained, with the help of HaShem, in the portion Re'eh, concerning the verse ״לֹא תֹאכְלֶנּוּ לְמַעַן יִיטַב לְךָ וּלְבָנֶיךָ אַחֲרֶיךָ״ (Deuteronomy 12:25), for blood produces cruelty and it also spreads to his sons. Therefore it does not say ״אֶל אָבִיו״ but ״אֶל אֲבִיהֶם״ is written, to tell you that all three touch upon the honor of their father."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:50.125373058Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}